## What this tool does with Steinberg's software

Everything here works with a Dorico installation the user already has and has licensed:

- It speaks to the Remote Control WebSocket that Dorico exposes when the user turns it on in **Preferences >
  Remote Control**. That is a documented, user-enabled feature, used as intended.
- `file read` decodes `.dorico` files **the user owns**, reading them from disk. The format was reverse engineered
  for interoperability — to let a user's own scores be read by other tools — and the decoder is our own code.
- `tools/analyze_minidump.py` reads crash dumps of the user's own machine.
- Nothing here is a copy of Steinberg code, and no Steinberg binary, string table or other extracted data is
  distributed in this repository.

## Reverse engineering, and the vocabulary question

Commands, parameter names and the values their enums take were learned by observing the application: its
`application.log` (which echoes every command Dorico performs), the command catalogue it publishes over the API,
and the XML files Dorico itself exports. Some of the enum spellings in `EnumVocabulary` were seen in the
application's own strings.

Nothing extracted in that way is distributed here, and there is nothing to extract: the values the CLI validates
are a short built-in table of the parameters it actually sends, each one either read back from Dorico's status or
taken from Dorico's own command catalogue. No extractor script ships, no per-user vocabulary file is generated, and
no string table from Steinberg's binary is stored in this repository or in the released executable.

## Prior work

The Remote Control protocol was documented first by [Dorico.Net](https://github.com/scott-janssens/Dorico.Net)
(MIT, © its contributors). No source code from that project is copied here: this repository's client was written
from scratch, and Dorico.Net was used as a reference for message ids and parameter and enum spellings. Thanks
also to the Dorico forum threads quoted in `docs/scripting.md`, which is where the shape of the Lua layer is
described.

## Files you bring

